Since .NET 8 the standard approach is `IExceptionHandler` together with `AddProblemDetails`, instead of scattering `try/catch` through controllers.

```csharp
public sealed class GlobalExceptionHandler(ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext ctx, Exception ex, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        logger.LogError(ex, "Unhandled exception on {Path}", ctx.Request.Path);

        var problem = new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = ex is NotFoundException ? StatusCodes.Status404NotFound
                                             : StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError,
            Title = "An error occurred",
            Instance = ctx.Request.Path,
        };

        ctx.Response.StatusCode = problem.Status.Value;
        await ctx.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problem, ct);
        return true;   // false -> pass to the next handler
    }
}

builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
// ...
app.UseExceptionHandler();
```

Key points:
- `ProblemDetails` is **RFC 7807** — clients get a fixed structure (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`) to parse instead of a different shape per endpoint.
- You can register **multiple handlers**; they run in registration order, and returning `false` defers to the next one.
- Handlers are **singleton** — do not inject scoped services directly.
- Never put `ex.ToString()` in the response in production; log the stack trace server-side and return a `traceId` the client can quote.

Compared with a hand-written `try/catch` middleware: same idea, but with built-in `ProblemDetails` integration and handler chaining
